I've traced the incident to a single fault: ts-station-service lost its pod for about a minute, and the failure spread up the travel chain to the user-facing dashboard.

```json
{
  "root_causes": [
    {
      "service": "ts-station-service",
      "fault_kind": "pod_failure",
      "evidence": [
        {"kind": "log", "sql": "SELECT service_name, time, message FROM abnormal_logs WHERE service_name='ts-basic-service' AND level='SEVERE' ORDER BY time LIMIT 5", "claim": "ts-basic-service SEVERE logs: connection refused calling ts-station-service /stations/idlist during the outage."},
        {"kind": "trace", "sql": "SELECT attr_k8s_pod_name pod, min(time) t0, max(time) t1, count(*) n FROM abnormal_traces WHERE service_name='ts-station-service' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2", "claim": "Station pod j66bn stops emitting at 09:38:38; replacement pod p9g9v only starts emitting at 09:39:36."},
        {"kind": "trace", "sql": "SELECT date_trunc('second', time) s, count(*) n, sum(CASE WHEN attr_status_code='Error' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) err FROM abnormal_traces WHERE service_name='ts-basic-service' AND time BETWEEN '2025-09-06 09:38:30' AND '2025-09-06 09:40:00' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1", "claim": "ts-basic-service errors cluster in the 09:38:38–09:39:35 gap and stop once station returns."}
      ]
    }
  ],
  "propagation": [
    {"from": "ts-station-service", "to": "ts-basic-service", "evidence": [{"kind": "trace", "sql": "SELECT p.service_name caller, c.service_name callee, count(*) n, sum(CASE WHEN c.attr_status_code='Error' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) callee_err FROM abnormal_traces c JOIN abnormal_traces p ON c.parent_span_id=p.span_id AND c.trace_id=p.trace_id WHERE p.service_name='ts-basic-service' AND c.service_name='ts-station-service' GROUP BY 1,2", "claim": "ts-basic-service calls to ts-station-service; basic's own 500 errors are logged as connection refused to station."}]},
